#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# inverse_radon_transform

#% text
# `inverse_radon_transform(g, [p, phi], [x, y])` gives the function `f(x, y)` whose Radon transform (its integrals over the lines `x cos(phi) + y sin(phi) = p`) is `g(p, phi)`; `inverse_radon_transform(g, [p, phi], [x0, y0])` its value at a point.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Transforms `e^(-p^2) Q(p, cos(phi), sin(phi))` with a polynomial `Q` invert to `e^(-x^2 - y^2) P(x, y)`: the coefficients of `P` come from the transforms of `e^(-r^2) x^i y^j` (Gaussian moments in the rotated frame), matched at sample lines.

#% item
# A point mass `dirac_delta(p - a cos(phi) - b sin(phi))` inverts to `dirac_delta(x - a, y - b)`; the chord lengths `2 sqrt(r^2 - p^2)` on `|p| < r` invert to the disk of radius `r`.

#% item
# The convention is that of `radon_transform`: `inverse_radon_transform(radon_transform(f, [x, y], [p, phi]), [p, phi], [x, y]) = f`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
inverse_radon_transform(e^(-p^2), [p, phi], [x, y])
#% output
#= exp(-x^2 - y^2)/pi^(1/2)

#% cell
inverse_radon_transform((cos(phi)^2 + 2*p^2*sin(phi)^2)*e^(-p^2), [p, phi], [x, y])
#% output
#= 2*exp(-x^2 - y^2)*y^2/pi^(1/2)

#% cell
inverse_radon_transform(p*(2*p^2 - 3)*sin(phi)*sin(2*phi)*e^(-p^2), [p, phi], [x, y])
#% output
#= 2*x*(-1 + 2*y^2)*exp(-x^2 - y^2)/pi^(1/2)

#% cell
inverse_radon_transform(p*cos(phi)*e^(-p^2), [p, phi], [1/3, 1/5])
#% output
#= 1/(3*exp(34/225)*pi^(1/2))

#% cell
inverse_radon_transform(dirac_delta(p - a*cos(phi) - b*sin(phi)), [p, phi], [x, y])
#% output
#= dirac_delta(x - a, y - b)

#% text
# Round trip:

#% cell
radon_transform(inverse_radon_transform((1 + 2*p^2)*e^(-p^2), [p, phi], [x, y]), [x, y], [p, phi])
#% output
#= (1 + 2*p^2)*exp(-p^2)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `radon_transform`, `fourier_transform`
